<worksheet>
  <title>Quiz: Spanning Trees Attempt 1</title>
  
  <introduction>
    <p>
      Name: <fillin characters="45"/>
    </p>
    <assemblage>
      <title>Algorithm 188: Kruskal's algorithm</title>
      <p><ol>
        <li>
          <p>Select the cheapest unused edge in the graph.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>Repeat step 1, adding the cheapest unused edge, unless adding the edge would create a circuit.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>Repeat until a spanning tree is formed.</p>
        </li>
      </ol></p>
    </assemblage>
  </introduction>

  <exercise workspace="0.1in">
    <statement>
      <p>For each graph below (left and right), determine whether it is a tree. Explain each decision. If a graph is not a tree, identify a cycle or explain why it is disconnected.</p>
      <sidebyside widths="38% 38%" margins="12%">
        <image source="images/quiz-a-q1-left" width="80%">
          <shortdescription>Graph with vertices A, B, C, D, E, F, G.</shortdescription>
          <description>
            <p>Edges join these vertex pairs: AF, AG, BC, CF, DF, EF.</p>
          </description>
        </image>
        <image source="images/quiz-a-q1-right" width="80%">
          <shortdescription>Graph with vertices A, B, C, D, E.</shortdescription>
          <description>
            <p>Edges join these vertex pairs: AC, BC, BD, BE, CD.</p>
          </description>
        </image>
      </sidebyside>
    </statement>
  </exercise>
  
  <exercise workspace="0.1in">
    <statement>
      <p>Draw a spanning tree for the graph below. You may mark the retained edges directly on the graph or redraw the tree. List the edges in your tree.</p>
      <image source="images/quiz-a-q2" width="25%">
        <shortdescription>Graph with vertices A, B, C, D, E.</shortdescription>
        <description>
          <p>Edges join these vertex pairs: AB, AC, AD, AE, BC, CD, CE, DE. Some pairs have parallel edges; a spanning tree can use at most one edge from each pair.</p>
        </description>
      </image>
    </statement>
  </exercise>
  
  <exercise workspace="0.5in">
    <statement>
      <p>Use Kruskal's algorithm to find a minimum-weight spanning tree for the graph below. Mark or draw its edges, list them in the order selected, and state the total weight.</p>
      <image source="images/quiz-a-q3" width="40%">
        <shortdescription>Weighted graph with vertices A, B, C, D, E.</shortdescription>
        <description>
          <p>Edge weights: AB = 1, AC = 11, AD = 5, AE = 8, BC = 10, BD = 3, BE = 2, CD = 7, CE = 4, DE = 9.</p>
        </description>
      </image>
    </statement>
  </exercise>
  
